  "raw_answer": "C1|Class I antiarrhythmic drugs are sodium channel blockers divided into three subgroups (IA, IB, IC) based on their effect on action potential duration: IA lengthens the action potential, IB shortens it, and IC has little effect on action potential duration|Passage 4|\nC2|Class II antiarrhythmic drugs are beta‑adrenergic blockers that decrease sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodal activity by lowering cAMP and calcium currents, suppress abnormal pacemaker activity by reducing the phase‑4 slope, and increase PR interval|Passage 2|\nC3|Class III antiarrhythmic drugs block delayed rectifier potassium currents (I_K), slowing phase 3 repolarization, which prolongs action potential duration, effective refractory period, and QT interval|Passage 3|",
